course: dictionary
lesson: 1262
title: Burrow, a home and a verb
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A burrow is a hole or tunnel in the ground that an animal digs to live or hide in. Burrow can also be a verb: to burrow is to dig a hole, or to push yourself deep into something soft. Look at the words around it to find the right meaning.
explain: «A rabbit thumps its back foot to warn other rabbits of danger, and the thud travels through the ground into the burrow.» The words ground and rabbits are clues. A burrow must be a hole in the ground where a rabbit can hide, so the noun meaning fits.
explain: «She burrowed under the blankets.» Here burrowed is a verb. Try your meaning in the sentence: she pushed herself deep under the blankets to get warm and hidden. That fits, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: A burrow is a hole or tunnel in the ground that an animal digs to live or hide in.
fact: To burrow means to dig a hole or tunnel, or to push yourself deep into something soft to get comfortable or hide.
fact: Earthworms, moles, badgers, rabbits and wombats all live in burrows.
fact: Burrows keep animals safe from predators and bad weather.
fact: «Marmots» whistle when they see danger, and then everyone dives into the burrows.
exercise: «She burrowed under the blankets to keep warm.» What does burrowed mean here? | Sang loudly | Ran away quickly | Dug a hole in the garden | *Pushed herself deep into something soft to hide or get comfortable
exercise: «The fox ran into its burrow to hide.» What is a burrow? | A loud whistle that warns of danger | A soft blanket for sleeping | *A hole or tunnel in the ground where an animal lives or hides | A tall tree with a nest in its top
exercise: «A rabbit thumps its back foot to warn other rabbits of danger, and the thud travels through the ground into the burrow.» Which word tells you where a burrow is? | *ground | thumps | danger | rabbit
test: «The marmots whistled, and everyone dived into the burrows.» What does burrows mean? | *Holes or tunnels in the ground where animals live or hide | Tall grass where animals run | Fast races across the meadow | Loud whistles at the sky
test: Tom says burrow only means a rabbit's home. What is the best answer? | *A burrow is a hole an animal digs, and burrow can also be a verb meaning to push deep into something soft | Tom is right, it only means a rabbit's home | Burrow means a loud noise made by an animal | Burrow means a large bird that flies at night
exam: Which sentence uses burrow as a verb meaning to search deep inside something? | Owls are wild hunters, not teachers. | The marmots whistled when the eagle came. | *She burrowed into her bag to find her keys. | The rabbit's burrow was under the oak tree.
